tion "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 7+10\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =24+28-80 \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab5b1de-870f-5536-aa90-1a17bff6118e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497713} :answer-field/id #uuid "59cf03c7-0bcc-556e-b453-60d46073703a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9d2827b-b813-5ca9-99e1-cb629b5135ae" :question/problem "If $a=⟨4,11,4⟩$ and $b=⟨8,-11,3⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8+11\\cdot (-11)+4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =32-121+12 \\\\  & =-77.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b5771e-881c-5dc7-bd47-631de07881b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-31.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497699} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3cec3a-81a2-544d-8588-a3c2224df3cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ef62ae0b-9434-50fd-9674-89052980205a" :question/problem "If $a=⟨3.9,4.9,-5.9⟩$ and $b=⟨-5.6,1.8,3.2⟩,$ then $a\\cdot b=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =3.9\\cdot (-5.6)+4.9\\cdot 1.8+(-5.9)\\cdot 3.2 \\\\  & =-21.84+8.82-18.88 \\\\  & =-31.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c97c988-3336-5cff-9def-ec7413102202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6(u+w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498757} {:answer/id #uuid "1c002a85-7881-5d55-b3a3-c0858f2a52d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(u+v-w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498789} {:answer/id #uuid "449afe6a-6893-523f-8969-4d9facf23e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6(v-w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498886} {:answer/id #uuid "5760564c-6d75-5aa4-a9ab-7873348de3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(u-w-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498930} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc9fd42-c83a-5ec5-bb24-9d73b0ba363e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(v-w)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498757} :answer-field/id #uuid "219a7147-3bde-5244-974f-5c2639b194f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "21557ab9-79b3-55a0-a104-51c3d273599e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161868" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le \\frac{2x-y}{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\frac{y}{2}\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=6.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |6|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,6(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d194298-7b9f-5375-a63e-abe0fd00183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498909} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b43ea-6957-5564-abd5-fa3a9b69baee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499007} {:answer/id #uuid "83dea68e-c1c5-56fd-83b2-641ed6509139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499027} {:answer/id #uuid "d1084bbd-c9b1-5b9e-aba8-ccbe75dad070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499193} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d04b97-b925-5d6c-8da1-814296c0c804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499202} :answer-field/id #uuid "000a0f4a-ae7b-5f51-a759-311c42f36adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "310694c7-b1be-5cf8-b20f-09c0a8e64636" :question/math-academy-id "q-162638" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+4y)\\,i-(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y)\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field does have a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-4{x}^{2}y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x-(4{x}^{2}y+1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17418eda-e303-583e-abb8-8163f34cf65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498779} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f2bf6-1e84-5da8-8223-f7a8617abe63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa17f5a-3932-5a85-be4a-9e7f8aca50c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499103} {:answer/id #uuid "af9d5967-0e62-5e9c-80be-bb9563606efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499116} {:answer/id #uuid "e507ffc4-50dc-56ba-8db0-2ce573a2305a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} :answer-field/id #uuid "06575685-8b6e-5313-b708-d919a2eb705b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5e32ec93-9a95-5b63-b1e8-8733a4d5c7af" :question/math-academy-id "q-82910" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curves $(x+y)^{3}-y=0$ and $(x+y)^{2}-y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{3}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{3}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{3} \\\\ v &={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{3}\\le v\\le {u}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8d2549c665a918cc8d69c69136fcb49e654ebb9aa5e53611155798e791b36a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{u}^{3})du \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9b4ef9-8eb6-5c5c-b433-5ffc4c7236ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498824} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8c6c00-5b20-53bc-acb7-b0b31ab2298d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{12}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498947} {:answer/id #uuid "88676aa6-a08b-583a-a6f9-2618bd726361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499034} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e226dc-7e0d-534a-9b5e-f226b2636d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499239} {:answer/id #uuid "f378af41-a686-5292-9362-123eea7e70ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499239} :answer-field/id #uuid "fecc3609-91c7-5f66-a205-ec1648ad9bd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77ed6de7-60cd-57e5-95ef-8b1b61a0f9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-160638" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{{e}^{x}},\\,y=\\frac{12}{{e}^{x}},\\,2y-1=0,\\,2y-5=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}y=5,\\,{e}^{x}y=12,\\,2y=1,\\,2y=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x}y,\\,v=2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={e}^{x}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}y=5\\,\\text{ }\\,⟹\\,u=5 \\\\  & {e}^{x}y=12\\,⟹\\,u=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 2y=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,5\\le u\\le 12,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/61/615d6822e60e9b5ff646497e14df1468e1fd09ee67fab5c334b635d130f16f14.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x}y & {e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}y \\\\  & =2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{12}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1784c964-2bd8-551a-9e62-6cb6f2560ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498780} {:answer/id #uuid "255b1548-0862-5d3a-8e4b-d22ea5aa0f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498812} {:answer/id #uuid "4f74f069-41cb-54fc-8a9a-08d22d7c1865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498916} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9780c4-afc1-59e7-ac65-84b80ad23ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499011} {:answer/id #uuid "903bd708-b320-50fc-b991-e52faae4df28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499042}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499011} :answer-field/id #uuid "2030cf44-2880-55da-b8da-7f5dffbc7894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "787730fc-5924-57cd-ac3a-f6e87e085583" :question/math-academy-id "q-161893" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n∭(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1-(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{u{e}^{v}}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot {[{e}^{v}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (e-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2837348b-6d6d-5f9c-b59a-17f08f8c704b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498816} {:answer/id #uuid "2f63601b-8c18-5c62-9c1d-b23197c0248e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy{sin}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498839} {:answer/id #uuid "61678505-c4a2-51bf-9209-a1b476663d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498952} {:answer/id #uuid "9c7cb5ce-c76c-525b-952b-985fcb08f78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499060} {:answer/id #uuid "e371cf2c-6265-557b-893f-ac88cb328896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$F$ has no stream function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499241} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a53c735-9b72-59d0-8d36-96505245d570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8662a311-148d-52aa-9581-67da3a804b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-162537" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eccd188-ea83-597f-abf8-03ad9133061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b8/b83a05a3fd79bf42ef45a16565194bf104214647a06144641e9b7bd66613ffaf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498836} {:answer/id #uuid "57f76c7f-ba08-5d98-870b-229dd3bdfcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f4/f4ad2cbbaccce4edae6e7e4901c6269a91aae1b364beba9b8bcc419d8e32c0e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498932} {:answer/id #uuid "675416c8-3285-5ede-b099-55462b554c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a2/a2fd8b46cf90aa751bfbd5f6c39257b8097a4898a5f5807bf5876a462d925fb2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498963} {:answer/id #uuid "9eedb55c-c4b8-5b99-a700-dafb5e8a0479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2a/2a4e9e152f2da6d5b254cc6f3eb6f6c6d71dcf96e139116753587f7663d39b2f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499067} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d994a1-a196-5c00-a356-dbb042053b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/97/9708321a993fcee685037db3ff120203d00a0c62892cf3712139e9afaa0426fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499121}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498836} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbb9fdb9-29b3-51cf-8b8d-3e71313f6843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8e84ccf0-2025-55a1-91ce-ccafeeadc53e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162621" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x-1)i-y\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(x-1)i-y\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=x-1$ and $Q(x,y)=-y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=x-1,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=xy-y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=0$ or $g(y)=-y,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=xy-y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=xy-y$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ xy-y & =c \\\\ y(x-1) & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=1$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/dc/dcf779041bbae1ce0a6c89042277b4dc33bb1eabe16d42a7824945a118951e0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12666955-acda-50b1-a759-415719031788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498766} {:answer/id #uuid "33260567-19af-5f87-8391-f7b7b20532c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498847} {:answer/id #uuid "a6520622-46f1-5688-af38-61a3ff107da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499095} {:answer/id #uuid "c17ed11d-1615-505b-9ee3-f62b086faf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499157} {:answer/id #uuid "faf10f5c-fe15-5f32-843c-38243f61e787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499095} :answer-field/id #uuid "772a5b09-0846-503c-9495-bc958a42c8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "92990ecc-5ba0-56e3-944d-6e84f299eb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-162210" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-{e}^{x}\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z{e}^{-x}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-{e}^{x},\\,v=y,\\,w=z{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & 0 & {e}^{-x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ -z{e}^{-x} & {e}^{-x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "040577a1-f488-586f-a244-5ac52452a051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498738} {:answer/id #uuid "134f7fbf-b645-5f5e-a680-2cf1d5fc853c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498772} {:answer/id #uuid "7649b34b-2218-533c-b3c5-146847fddc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498997} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d46a27-dd36-5701-8dd1-b3331e4e3270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499086} {:answer/id #uuid "e83877aa-f978-5e05-a20d-0069c96a2685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499086} :answer-field/id #uuid "612abc3f-006a-54a9-ba2c-ed70e2955dbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad40770e-3352-5877-ade7-ccfd965beccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162185" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-1\\le {x}^{2}-y\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\,x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-y,\\,v=x,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b1f9282-40f6-534c-9cb9-df688ee64de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498822} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2fce83-b1b2-5397-8cab-576be2461576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499059} {:answer/id #uuid "a70e370b-8012-5ba7-8c81-0ea9db91d6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499097} {:answer/id #uuid "d553a226-27d4-508f-a4c8-4c5c6f0cd0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$  and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499207} {:answer/id #uuid "e67fa0c9-ee34-5eea-a810-dee87b7233d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-2)$  and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498822} :answer-field/id #uuid "f38fea1a-0a62-5cc4-9aaa-d50dbb5879df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b5a12ff0-24e0-5963-9f16-1e9a01270fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-72439" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-xy=x+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-xy & =x+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-xy-x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-xy-x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-xy-x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-xy-x)j \\\\  & =(2y-x)\\,i+(y+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2y-x)\\,i+(y+1)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2y-x & =0 \\\\ x & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2y$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-(2y)y-(2y) & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ -{y}^{2}-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=-1$, we get $x=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ba8864-9639-569c-861f-6e638330546b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498857} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5ff22c-2612-54bb-bf13-52b4667b899d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499006} {:answer/id #uuid "9d918342-1efd-5f5f-a70c-62d3002a283b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499065} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fcce98-7907-5c3f-8f29-c741eb4b7cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499135} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1550ba-8b0e-51be-878a-b2b84082b1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499172} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7015fd4-ab37-5009-8ea6-02df343db6e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b96af04a-99a9-59d6-b4b4-fac54542ff08" :question/math-academy-id "q-65205" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x{e}^{y}-1=xy.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}-1 & =xy \\\\ x{e}^{y}-xy & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}-xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}-xy)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}-xy)j \\\\  & =(x{e}^{y}-x)i-({e}^{y}-y)j \\\\ t(1,0) & =((1){e}^{0}-(1))i-({e}^{0}-(0))j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d03c985-35da-5986-ad82-1850226331bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498827} {:answer/id #uuid "2d390a46-6a6d-580f-aedb-aa1ff6dfc987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498830} {:answer/id #uuid "50ac5137-180c-55d6-9f11-2a2575b2db27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498919} {:answer/id #uuid "82a30966-fd2e-5ead-9908-43144c65211f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499023} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c1e3bd-329c-592b-a22f-764ef4182668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498827} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cb50c3b-df4c-5b33-bad4-52581805f032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c7c0eb25-897e-5093-b34f-0455f3db55f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162615" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=({y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y)\\,i+({x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2})\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+4{y}^{4}\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i-3y\\,j$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-6xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(-6xy)+6xy=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field II doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =16{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+16{y}^{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+(-3)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1052d2-3a28-5ca0-9e37-9f2ccf48871c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498760} {:answer/id #uuid "0f687a71-756e-59f6-bc52-a42d2a1ff16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498761} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac23b03-c1ad-5ab7-be63-a35fc34705f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498787} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf883f8-d823-5d14-9a1b-8d3c64093c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498942} {:answer/id #uuid "fac357f6-d241-5f53-aa60-7685a0cfadeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7da737-6ab6-5dc3-a356-5e38af59041a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ea587ab0-d077-5468-b262-1697c88c7094" :question/math-academy-id "q-72389" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2{e}^{xy}=x+y+1.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{xy} & =x+y+1 \\\\ 2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(0,1)={f}_{y}(0,1)\\,i-{f}_{x}(0,1)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x{e}^{xy}-1)\\,i-(2y{e}^{xy}-1)\\,j \\\\ t(0,1) & =(2(0){e}^{(0)(1)}-1)\\,i-(2(1){e}^{(0)(1)}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i-j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c2467c5-2c2d-5b5d-bc2b-28eb5cd0d0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ef/ef27d204f39550af6a529640dd3e3c24f13dcd7c7d9631c5fd6d2a6d1e47ebcf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498906} {:answer/id #uuid "56d8e193-cdfa-5f8f-af06-4ddefe6b4ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/91/91e7124ef57462e9d8e0045582ccc8149608c687f3a822071345fa5b5f532cdd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498928} {:answer/id #uuid "66c14112-d0b5-53d0-8392-f270d6eec5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/42/42e1190d821286b8f66f39850d6c895b2801ef3e3f736d4e4df80df2e6ce513d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498961} {:answer/id #uuid "8332446a-894a-5efa-b549-105c9d96f0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d0/d060084a75147a69ffd6a74da31bfad2f9ad807dedddb5ee3b8fc20993432f9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499025} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc9ec84-1b7b-5e9c-a576-3127b03fef54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2a/2ad340e6cd5a65e7f563ac898e7a56805a6d61e53ece75a6a4469031c60a62a5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499220} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c353f18-26f0-57c7-b34f-03eb3c1ed93a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "faed1d58-d701-5e29-bb64-3a9beae53a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162567" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f2f77c6c2047dcd3f542fc0f640a68b45a4d40784d0e7eaacdcadb002c79de5.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following shows the streamlines of this vector field?" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nOf the given options, the only level curves tangential to the vector field at every point are shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd47775ffb4cba4cc01dde0bf404dca12bdeecc24e2f58639c04f1feda8293ee.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0c0406-ae8d-54c8-b629-8afd582156ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499678} {:answer/id #uuid "23b36d38-920a-5a19-90af-0d58ea66b6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499733} {:answer/id #uuid "4e379d7d-a44b-5b19-ae49-edc70a8dad28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-\\sqrt[3]{4},\\sqrt[3]{4})$ and $(\\sqrt[3]{4},-\\sqrt[3]{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499802} {:answer/id #uuid "930ec2dc-3268-5e8c-bb20-3557f9ac1938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-\\sqrt[3]{4},\\sqrt[3]{4})$ and $(-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499942} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6ba7c0-fd84-5eed-9805-7ee46c6511bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-\\sqrt[3]{4},\\sqrt[3]{4})$ and $(2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499678} :answer-field/id #uuid "956e93fd-6df8-5b5f-8f5c-3b6f83220074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a5f3b132-60d1-51a8-b2e5-d8b20ae5add9" :question/math-academy-id "q-65209" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $3y{x}^{2}=48+3x{y}^{2}$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i-j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{x}^{2} & =48+3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2} & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=48.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-6xy)i-(6xy-3{y}^{2})j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot (i-j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (i-j) & =0 \\\\ ((3{x}^{2}-6xy)i-(6xy-3{y}^{2})j)\\cdot (i-j) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6xy+6xy-3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-y)(x+y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y$ or $x=-y.$ Substituting $x=y$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{x}^{2} & =48+3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y{y}^{2} & =48+3y{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{3} & =48+3{y}^{3} \\\\ 0 & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we get a false statement, we conclude that does not exist points of the form $(x,x)$ on the curve. Substituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{x}^{2} & =48+3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y(-y)^{2} & =48+3(-y){y}^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{3} & =48-3{y}^{3} \\\\ 6{y}^{3} & =48 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =8 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=2$, we get $x=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "620afa66-2c6c-57b7-a3ae-d62a79f7f8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{u}^{3}{v}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500671} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebe624d-df33-5b1a-ac75-393b05dce501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{u}^{3}{v}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500692} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd3bc80-2dcb-5963-9f05-c0a968725eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}(3u+2v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500784} {:answer/id #uuid "c2689dcd-d1d7-54f8-a7ca-f907d83e79ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}(uv+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500873} {:answer/id #uuid "f99ddd32-d363-5197-81ba-b8f06c983f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{u}^{2}{v}^{2}(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500671} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fefe9c8-5fc6-5bbb-bfe2-94890c45b24c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2343ccc6-302e-57d1-903e-91d537e6ff5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161385" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨-2x,\\,-2y,\\,-z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{3},\\,{v}^{3},\\,uv⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-3{v}^{3},\\,-3{u}^{3},\\,9{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-3{v}^{3},\\,-3{u}^{3},\\,9{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨-2x,\\,-2y,\\,-z⟩{|}_{x={u}^{3}\\,y={v}^{3}\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨-2{u}^{3},\\,-2{v}^{3},\\,-uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-2{u}^{3},\\,-2{v}^{3},\\,-uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-3{v}^{3},\\,-3{u}^{3},\\,9{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =(-2{u}^{3})\\cdot (-3{v}^{3})+(-2{v}^{3})\\cdot (-3{u}^{3})+(-uv)\\cdot 9{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{v}^{3}+6{u}^{3}{v}^{3}-9{u}^{3}{v}^{3} \\\\  & =3{u}^{3}{v}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,3{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c949071-0d3c-54bb-9dc4-96b0d8539778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500621} {:answer/id #uuid "610bdf54-043a-5740-ad3a-0dec8d2b57b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500665} {:answer/id #uuid "663bf8f7-c948-5768-ae58-7d976d853146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500680} {:answer/id #uuid "cee0c053-9570-5242-9c89-c00687d42bb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500911} {:answer/id #uuid "dde36f82-c9e7-56b6-95a2-ab881de27e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500939} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9e74ba-160b-54c7-b340-b16d836e9f06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ea3ea5b4-3a71-55fd-af96-eb417a578734" :question/math-academy-id "q-160895" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(0,0),$ $(2,-2),$ $(3,-1),$ and $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y){e}^{x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(0,0) & =(0-0,0+0) \\\\  & =(0,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-2) & =(2-(-2),2+(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-1) & =(3-(-1),3+(-1)) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,1) & =(1-1,1+1) \\\\  & =(0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0b/0b23c6463447aa1fb64bdeeca689a40711f8dad59b348bb7943c2b9d2de7e8fc.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}u{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =4({e}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0edfd29e-3a12-5f5c-aa4d-c9d232e9d8ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501491} {:answer/id #uuid "10477c22-e6c0-5396-865c-7efb2e452797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501495} {:answer/id #uuid "1354d1db-51ab-5f5c-944c-79110ffcc91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501502} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba7e918-0922-576c-91b5-2e38e5a1b977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501571} {:answer/id #uuid "84d571a0-192f-5d6b-bfdc-6b4cf1be9b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501720} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a680a9f-c944-5dc6-b6be-a4a3dda024b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3d5c488c-135d-5c5b-b6fb-380729a9e440" :question/math-academy-id "q-161886" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(3,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-v \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-1 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(3,1)} & =(3{u}^{2}-1)\\,{|}_{u=3\\,v=1} \\\\  & =3(3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d22642e-b561-5d45-9cb7-82757bfb8fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502275} {:answer/id #uuid "b819491c-4982-5837-bd63-cf7cbf0fcdca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502295} {:answer/id #uuid "c135f7e5-e0da-5c3a-b8ca-997219572036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502298} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5f2b0d-d9f2-5dd0-b7b2-49aff096e1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{900}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502301} {:answer/id #uuid "d166af1b-0cf9-5dec-8c87-fa08df1270f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502304}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502295} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d2e60a-0ce1-5292-a4eb-f3f5138e347e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0addcaab-466a-56b5-8876-89572e8523d0" :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $30$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{30}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ed88a5-aa38-56a7-b95a-b944cd36a716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502234} {:answer/id #uuid "5b95b006-a9ac-52fb-8a75-6890c1dfdee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{169}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502253} {:answer/id #uuid "8f65c1d8-6fe9-55f7-b4c4-b556b2ac3008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502277} {:answer/id #uuid "96d2bb9b-0bb4-5f8a-a1e1-3bdd110a0c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502280} {:answer/id #uuid "aeaf653e-f634-5a0b-a2cc-f8564a08c146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{8}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502280} :answer-field/id #uuid "68e3a191-17fa-5bc4-a6b8-eebf36366d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0dfdf152-882f-5405-a75a-91f741a85b30" :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $\\frac{13}{8}$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{13}{8}}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{8}{13}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a58e835-ab50-56ae-8566-617689b6a510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502221} {:answer/id #uuid "5698201f-ea9d-5953-83ea-2d7b87965ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{289}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502248} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfb674c-b2e2-5961-a534-b0dd8ca72e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502267} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc93a19-088f-56b8-b79c-ff0030dcb0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502273} {:answer/id #uuid "b14ca792-1cbd-5fff-b811-699f3c459eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502292} :answer-field/id #uuid "d942863d-0152-5457-b78e-77bdbc89987f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "102d558d-5f91-58de-9348-d0fc95d217f6" :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $\\frac{17}{8}$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{17}{8}}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{8}{17}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d08d89-8e62-5915-9b8d-f14f87d8031e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{11}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502249} {:answer/id #uuid "9b03178c-0041-55cd-886d-04c10cade62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{121}\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502282} {:answer/id #uuid "c9375296-3ecc-58bf-ad19-301250a2a129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502300} {:answer/id #uuid "d74e40a5-8093-57c5-b9ac-bf6432c1aa92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\overrightarrow{AB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502305} {:answer/id #uuid "da33ade1-72a3-5023-8758-7a8b976fed04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502249} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc62a26e-6ce2-5fad-aad7-1df602b6470a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "25392aed-7aa6-5736-bb26-a65857eaae39" :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $11$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{11}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}]}
